#!/usr/bin/env bash
# wt new <TICKET> [base-branch] [--integration] [--no-home]
# wt close <TICKET|PATH> [--force] [--dry-run]
# wt info <TICKET>
#
# The cheap path. One entry point for the whole worktree lifecycle, runnable by
# an agent that knows the ticket id and nothing else.
#
# ONE LINE, because this runs constantly
# --------------------------------------
# A successful `new` or `close` prints exactly one line, and the only thing on
# it that an agent cannot derive is the worktree PATH:
#
#   created worktree /repos/proj-T-2
#   closed worktree /repos/proj-T-2 (branch feature/T-2 kept)
#
# It used to print five keyed lines, four of which restated the first: LAUNCH is
# `<WORKTREE>/.skill-manager/bin/launch/claude` by construction, IF-EXIT-8 is
# `<WORKTREE>/.skill-manager/bin/cli/skill-manager home drift --ack` by
# construction, CLOSE is `wt close <TICKET>` — the command the caller just
# typed the other half of — and BRANCH is `feature/<TICKET>`. Four long
# absolute paths, on every worktree creation, for facts that follow from the
# fifth. IF-EXIT-8 was the worst of them: a remedy for a gate that had not
# fired, paid for on every run in which it never fires.
#
# They are not GONE, they are ON DEMAND. `wt info <TICKET>` prints the whole
# key set for a worktree that already exists, and `--verbose` prints it on the
# run that creates one. Both go through new-change.sh, which is still the only
# thing that computes them.
#
# The FAILING run is the one that may cost more, because the next move is not
# derivable from anything. It prints at most three short lines — what went
# wrong, one command that fixes it, and the log holding the rest — and it no
# longer dumps the child's whole stderr to the console. That dump was measured
# at 23 lines / 2.7 KB for a refused close, which is the case an agent meets
# most often and can least afford.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Creating a worktree correctly in this model used to require reading five
# pages first — CLAUDE.md, INTEGRATION.md, references/skill-homes.md,
# references/worktrees.md and git-issue-workflow's references/provision.md —
# because the rules that matter (which repo gets branched, where the worktree
# may live, that it needs its own Skill Manager home, which home that home must
# be a copy of, and that it may not be removed with `git worktree remove`) are
# spread across all of them. The observed failure mode is not that an agent
# reads them slowly. It is that it does not read them at all: it writes its own
# script, which knows none of those rules, and the damage shows up at teardown.
#
# So the entry point has to be cheaper than improvising one. That means:
#
#   * ONE name, three verbs, no options you have to know about.
#   * The output is the next move, and it is one line long.
#   * A failure names the one command that fixes it and a log holding the
#     reasoning. The reasoning is never printed.
#
# What this file is NOT
# ---------------------
# It holds no policy. Every decision — which repo, where the worktree goes,
# which home it is cloned from, whether it may be removed — stays in
# new-change.sh, close-change.sh and lib.sh, and the CONTRACT LINES ARE EMITTED
# BY THOSE SCRIPTS, not assembled here. That is deliberate and it is issue #50:
# the worktree a ticket names is computed by `ticket_worktree_path`, once, and
# a front door that re-derived it would be a second opinion about which home a
# worktree must close into. `wt` chooses a verb, suppresses prose, and forwards
# bytes.
#
# Which also answers git-issue-skill#4 — whether this primitive belongs in
# `git-issue` or in `skill-manager` rather than here. The interface a caller
# depends on is still a KEY set, not this path; what changed is WHERE it is
# printed. `wt info <TICKET>`, and `wt new --verbose`, print WORKTREE / BRANCH /
# LAUNCH / IF-EXIT-8 / CLOSE (/ PROPAGATE) exactly as before, and a refusal
# still prints FAILED / FIX — those are new-change.sh's and close-change.sh's
# own contract, unchanged, and a `wt` shipped from somewhere else is compatible
# if it forwards them. A caller that parsed the ONE-LINE summary is parsing
# prose and never could be relied on; `wt info` is what such a caller should
# read instead.
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"; . "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib.sh"

usage() {
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
usage: wt new   <TICKET> [base-branch] [--integration] [--no-home] [--verbose]
       wt close <TICKET|PATH> [--force] [--dry-run] [--verbose]
       wt info  <TICKET>

  new     Create the worktree for TICKET AND its own Skill Manager home, in the
          repo you are standing in, and leave it launchable.
  close   Tear that worktree down through the close-out gate, which refuses
          while its home still holds work that removing it would destroy.
  info    Print the full key set for a worktree that already exists. Creates
          and removes nothing.

A successful new or close prints ONE line, and the path on it is the only fact
that does not follow from convention:

  created worktree <path>
  closed worktree <path> (branch <branch> kept)

A refusal prints what went wrong, one command to run, and a log:

  error creating worktree: <one line>
  fix: <command>
  log: <path>

Everything else is on demand: `wt info <TICKET>` (or `--verbose`) prints
WORKTREE / BRANCH / LAUNCH / IF-EXIT-8 / CLOSE / PROPAGATE, and --verbose,
--force and --dry-run print the underlying script's own output as well.
references/worktrees.md explains why each key is what it is.

HOW LONG `new` TAKES, AND HOW TO WAIT FOR IT. Nearly all of it is copying the
Skill Manager home, so the cost tracks the SOURCE HOME and not the repo.
Measured on this machine: ~48 s against an 18-unit / 852 MB project home
(29 s of that is the file copy itself), a few seconds against a small one.
Nothing is printed until it is done. If it may outlast your timeout, do not
poll — `wt` prints, on stderr, one line naming a log the moment the run passes
WT_PROGRESS_AFTER seconds (default 25; set 0 to silence it), and `tail -f` on
that file shows the phases live. `close` is seconds; it runs no clone.
EOF
}

VERB="${1:-}"; [ $# -gt 0 ] && shift || true
# `info` is new-change.sh in its read-only mode, not a fourth script: the paths
# it prints are `ticket_worktree_path`'s answer, and a front door that recomputed
# them would be the second opinion issue #50 is about.
INFO_FLAG=""
case "$VERB" in
  new)   TARGET_SCRIPT="$SCRIPT_DIR/new-change.sh" ;;
  close) TARGET_SCRIPT="$SCRIPT_DIR/close-change.sh" ;;
  info)  TARGET_SCRIPT="$SCRIPT_DIR/new-change.sh"; INFO_FLAG="--info" ;;
  ""|-h|--help|help) usage; [ -n "$VERB" ] && exit 0 || exit 1 ;;
  *)     usage; die_fix 1 "$SCRIPT_DIR/wt help" "unknown verb: $VERB (expected 'new', 'close' or 'info')" ;;
esac

# --verbose is this file's own; everything else is forwarded verbatim, so `wt`
# never has to be taught an option the underlying script grows.
VERBOSE=0
ARGS=()
for a in "$@"; do
  case "$a" in
    --verbose|-v) VERBOSE=1 ;;
    *) ARGS+=("$a") ;;
  esac
done
[ "${#ARGS[@]}" -gt 0 ] || { usage; die_fix 1 "$SCRIPT_DIR/wt help" "wt $VERB needs a ticket id"; }

# `new-change.sh --quiet` suppresses its narration and NOT its refusals; the
# contract is unaffected either way. close-change.sh is left as it is — its
# output is the gate's verdict, which is the answer rather than an explanation
# of one — and is captured here so that a clean run prints only the contract.
#
# A scalar, not an empty array. macOS ships bash 3.2, where `"${A[@]}"` on an
# EMPTY array is an unbound-variable error under `set -u` — verified on
# 3.2.57 — so the close path would die before running anything. There is
# exactly one flag and it has no spaces, so a word-split scalar is both correct
# and the shape that cannot regress here.
# A `case`, not an `&&` list: `[ a ] || [ b ] && c` under `set -u`/`set -e` is
# exactly the shape whose exemption rules nobody remembers correctly, and this
# file already carries one bash-3.2 scar.
QUIET_FLAG=""
case "$VERB" in new|info) QUIET_FLAG="--quiet" ;; esac

# A run that DISCARDS is never quiet. `close --force` succeeds — exit 0, the
# CLOSED / BRANCH / DELETE key set, and no FAILED, because the two key sets are
# exclusive and the operator asked for this one. But the gate's refusal is
# exactly the list of work being thrown away, and stderr is where close-change.sh
# puts it. Swallowing that on a successful run would trade one defect (both
# contracts printed) for a worse one (a teardown that destroys work and says
# nothing anywhere). So the one flag that means "destroy it anyway" also means
# "show me what you destroyed".
FORCED=0
DRY=0
if [ "$VERB" = close ]; then
  for a in "${ARGS[@]}"; do
    [ "$a" = "--force" ] && FORCED=1
    [ "$a" = "--dry-run" ] && DRY=1
  done
fi

# WHEN THE ONE-LINE SUMMARY IS NOT THE ANSWER.
#
# A SUCCESSFUL run prints one line, because `new` and `close` run constantly and
# the path is the only fact on them. Four invocations are not that, and all four
# ask for the full contract by construction:
#
#   --verbose   the operator asked to see the working
#   info        the whole verb IS the key set
#   --dry-run   the answer to "would this close cleanly" is CLEAN / CLOSE, not
#               a sentence about a teardown that did not happen
#   --force     a teardown that DESTROYS work. One line saying it succeeded is
#               precisely the wrong output: what was thrown away is the only
#               thing worth printing, and close-change.sh prints it. The flag
#               that means "destroy it anyway" also means "show me what you
#               destroyed" — unchanged from before.
#
# This is about the SUCCESS shape only. A FAILING run is compact whatever verb
# or flag produced it (below, and `--verbose` excepted): none of the four
# reasons above survives a run that did nothing — there is no contract to print,
# nothing was destroyed, and the operator's next move is the same three lines
# either way.
SUMMARIZE=1
[ "$VERBOSE" = 1 ] && SUMMARIZE=0
[ "$FORCED" = 1 ] && SUMMARIZE=0
[ "$DRY" = 1 ] && SUMMARIZE=0
[ "$VERB" = info ] && SUMMARIZE=0

# stderr to a file, stdout to a variable. Both, always: on success the stderr is
# noise an agent should not have to read past, and on failure it is the
# explanation and must not be lost — but it is NAMED rather than printed, which
# is the whole failure-path saving. Capturing stdout is what lets a child that
# died WITHOUT emitting a contract still be answered with one.
#
# The file therefore OUTLIVES a failing run. It used to be unlinked on exit,
# which is safe only because the whole thing was dumped to the console first;
# naming a log this script had already deleted would be the worst of both.
_WT_TMP="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"
ERRLOG="$(mktemp "${_WT_TMP%/}/wt-XXXXXX")"   # %/ — TMPDIR usually ends in one
mv "$ERRLOG" "$ERRLOG.log"                    # .log so the name says what it is
ERRLOG="$ERRLOG.log"
KEEP_ERRLOG=0

# ------------------------------------------------------- a run you can WAIT ON
#
# THE COST THIS FIXES IS NOT OUTPUT, IT IS LATENCY FORCING ROUND-TRIPS.
#
# `wt new` prints nothing until it finishes — the contract is emitted atomically
# at the end, which is exactly what makes the one-line summary possible. Measured
# against an 18-unit / 852 MB project home it ran for 151 s before this change
# and 48 s after it. Either number can exceed a caller's foreground timeout on a
# larger home, and when it does the caller must background the command and then
# POLL, and each poll re-sends the whole growing transcript. Two independent
# fresh-agent evals measured that loop at roughly a third of one agent's entire
# token spend; the other spent five extra round-trips looking for a way to wait
# at all. Neither cost was in the output.
#
# So the run is made WATCHABLE instead. `new-change.sh --log FILE` lets the
# CALLER choose the transcript path before the run starts, and this script picks
# one, hands it over, and — if and only if the run is still going after
# WT_PROGRESS_AFTER seconds — prints ONE line naming it. `tail -f` on a file
# costs a constant; re-reading a transcript costs the transcript.
#
# WHY IT IS TIMED RATHER THAN UNCONDITIONAL. A successful `wt new` prints nothing
# on stderr, and that is an asserted property, not an accident: this whole file
# exists because the cheap path was not cheap. A run that finishes in three
# seconds never needed a way to be waited on, and paying a line for it on every
# invocation is the exact trade this file was written to undo. The line is
# therefore emitted only by a run long enough to have created the problem it
# solves. `WT_PROGRESS_AFTER=0` silences it; a small number makes it
# deterministic for a test.
PROGRESS_AFTER="${WT_PROGRESS_AFTER:-25}"
case "$PROGRESS_AFTER" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) die_fix 1 "WT_PROGRESS_AFTER=25 $SCRIPT_DIR/wt $VERB ${ARGS[*]}" \
                 "WT_PROGRESS_AFTER must be a whole number of seconds, got: $PROGRESS_AFTER" ;;
esac

# For `new`/`info` the watchable file is the child's own transcript, because
# --quiet sends its narration THERE and not to stderr. For `close` there is no
# such file — close-change.sh is not run quiet — so its stderr, which this
# script is already capturing, is the transcript.
#
# Two files, never one. Handing new-change.sh $ERRLOG as its --log would give
# the same file two independent write offsets: `2>"$ERRLOG"` here opens it
# truncating at offset 0, the child's own `exec 2>>` opens it appending, and its
# fd 3 (this script's stderr, inherited) still writes at offset 0. The failure
# path's own tail would overwrite the head of the log it is pointing at.
#
# Not on the --verbose path: there the child's own log line is printed to the
# terminal and the run is already live, so dictating the path would change what
# that mode reports for no gain.
RUNLOG=""
LOG_FLAG=""
if [ "$VERBOSE" = 0 ]; then
  case "$VERB" in
    new|info) RUNLOG="${ERRLOG%.log}-run.log"; LOG_FLAG="--log $RUNLOG" ;;
  esac
fi
WATCH_LOG="${RUNLOG:-$ERRLOG}"

KEEP_RUNLOG=0
cleanup() {
  [ "$KEEP_ERRLOG" = 1 ] || rm -f "$ERRLOG"
  [ -z "$RUNLOG" ] || [ "$KEEP_RUNLOG" = 1 ] || rm -f "$RUNLOG"
}
trap cleanup EXIT

RC=0
if [ "$VERBOSE" = 1 ]; then
  # `tee` so the operator watches it live AND the failure path can still read it.
  # Not backgrounded: --verbose is already live, so there is nothing a progress
  # line could add and a second stream would only interleave with the first.
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  OUT="$("$TARGET_SCRIPT" $INFO_FLAG $QUIET_FLAG $LOG_FLAG "${ARGS[@]}" 2> >(tee "$ERRLOG" >&2))" || RC=$?
else
  # Backgrounded so this shell keeps a clock. stdout goes to a file rather than a
  # command substitution for the same reason: `$( )` blocks, and a blocked shell
  # cannot say anything while it waits.
  OUTFILE="$ERRLOG.out"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  "$TARGET_SCRIPT" $INFO_FLAG $QUIET_FLAG $LOG_FLAG "${ARGS[@]}" >"$OUTFILE" 2>"$ERRLOG" &
  WT_CHILD=$!
  # Tenths, not seconds. `wt info` answers in about a tenth of a second and a
  # one-second poll would have made this file's cheapest verb ten times slower
  # to buy a line it never prints. The cost of the loop is one `sleep 0.1`.
  ticks=0; announced=0
  while kill -0 "$WT_CHILD" 2>/dev/null; do
    command sleep 0.1
    ticks=$((ticks + 1))
    waited=$((ticks / 10))
    if [ "$announced" = 0 ] && [ "$PROGRESS_AFTER" != 0 ] && [ "$ticks" -ge $((PROGRESS_AFTER * 10)) ]; then
      # ONE line, on stderr, naming a file that exists. It is deliberately not a
      # progress BAR: the phases are in the log, and a caller that wants them
      # runs the command this line hands it rather than being sent them.
      #
      # It does not START with `wt`, so that no line of documentation quoting
      # this output can be mistaken for an invocation. Every printed invocation
      # in this repo is an absolute path, and selftest.sh asserts it over the
      # pages by looking for a line that begins with a bare `wt <verb>`.
      printf 'still running after %ss (%s %s) — watch: tail -f %s\n' \
        "$waited" "$VERB" "${ARGS[0]}" "$WATCH_LOG" >&2
      # A file somebody has been told to watch is not deleted underneath them.
      # The EXIT trap unlinks these on a successful run, which is right for a run
      # nobody was told about and wrong for this one: the whole point of naming
      # it is that the caller reads it, and it is read after the run as often as
      # during it.
      if [ -n "$RUNLOG" ]; then KEEP_RUNLOG=1; else KEEP_ERRLOG=1; fi
      announced=1
    fi
  done
  wait "$WT_CHILD" || RC=$?
  OUT="$(command cat "$OUTFILE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  rm -f "$OUTFILE"
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------- reading a key
#
# The child's contract is the source of every value printed below. `wt` reads
# it; it never recomputes it. Anchored on `^KEY` followed by whitespace because
# CLOSE is a substring of CLOSED and an unanchored match reports the failure
# key set present on every success.
key() { printf '%s\n' "$OUT" | command sed -n "s/^$1[[:space:]][[:space:]]*//p" | command sed -n 1p; }

# --verbose is the one mode that keeps the old failing shape in full: the
# operator asked to watch it happen, and the child's stderr has already been
# tee'd to the terminal as it ran.
if [ "$RC" != 0 ] && [ "$VERBOSE" = 1 ]; then
  [ -z "$OUT" ] || printf '%s\n' "$OUT"
  case "$OUT" in
    *FAILED*) : ;;
    *) contract_fail "$SCRIPT_DIR/$(basename "$TARGET_SCRIPT") ${ARGS[*]}" \
         "$(command grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' "$ERRLOG" | command tail -n 1 || printf 'exit %s' "$RC")" ;;
  esac
  exit "$RC"
fi

if [ "$RC" = 0 ] && [ "$SUMMARIZE" = 0 ]; then
  # Pass-through: the contract on stdout, and the child's prose on stderr when
  # the caller asked for it or destroyed something with it.
  [ -z "$OUT" ] || printf '%s\n' "$OUT"
  if [ "$FORCED" = 1 ] && [ "$VERBOSE" != 1 ]; then
    printf '\n--- %s said:\n' "$(basename "$TARGET_SCRIPT")" >&2
    command cat "$ERRLOG" >&2
  fi
  exit 0
fi

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- one line
#
# WHAT and WHAT-IS-DERIVABLE-FROM-IT, and only the first is printed. The
# fallback when a key is missing is the child's whole contract rather than a
# confident sentence built from an empty string: a summary naming "" as the
# worktree is worse than five lines, because it reads as an answer.
if [ "$RC" = 0 ]; then
  case "$VERB" in
    new)
      WT_PATH="$(key WORKTREE)"
      if [ -n "$WT_PATH" ]; then printf 'created worktree %s\n' "$WT_PATH"
      else [ -z "$OUT" ] || printf '%s\n' "$OUT"; fi
      ;;
    close)
      WT_PATH="$(key CLOSED)"
      # The branch outlives the worktree, and which branch that is, is the one
      # fact left over that the path does not carry — `feature/<TICKET>` is only
      # the DEFAULT spelling. It costs a clause, not a line.
      WT_BR="$(key BRANCH)"; WT_BR="${WT_BR%% *}"
      # And the second thing that outlives the worktree: its HOME work, which
      # `home sync` moved exactly one tier up and no further. Also a clause, for
      # the same reason and one stronger — the worktree home is gone, the loss
      # appears in no diff, and a caller who is not told here is not told
      # anywhere. `%% *` again: the key's value carries the path first and its
      # explanation after, and the one line takes the path. (#8)
      WT_HOME="$(key HOME-WORK)"; WT_HOME="${WT_HOME%% *}"
      CLAUSE=""
      [ -z "$WT_BR" ]   || CLAUSE="branch $WT_BR kept"
      [ -z "$WT_HOME" ] || CLAUSE="${CLAUSE:+$CLAUSE; }home work went no further than $WT_HOME — push skill edits from there"
      if [ -n "$WT_PATH" ] && [ -n "$CLAUSE" ]; then
        printf 'closed worktree %s (%s)\n' "$WT_PATH" "$CLAUSE"
      elif [ -n "$WT_PATH" ]; then
        printf 'closed worktree %s\n' "$WT_PATH"
      else
        [ -z "$OUT" ] || printf '%s\n' "$OUT"
      fi
      ;;
  esac
  exit 0
fi

# The failing run. Three lines at most, and each one answers a question the
# agent has and cannot derive: what stopped it, what to run, where the
# reasoning is. The reasoning itself is NOT printed — that dump was the whole
# cost of a refusal, and it is still every byte of it, in the file named here.
#
# A child that refused through `die` rather than `die_fix` named no command, so
# the fallback is its last non-blank stderr line as the reason and a verbose
# re-run as the fix. Not a good fix, but a runnable one.
#
# The richest log there is. new-change.sh keeps its own transcript in a file and
# names it on the first line of its stderr; close-change.sh has no such file, so
# what this script captured IS the transcript. Preferring the child's means the
# named log holds the whole story rather than a tail of it.
#
# Resolved in THIS shell, never inside a command substitution: KEEP_ERRLOG is
# what stops the EXIT trap unlinking the very file the last line names, and an
# assignment made inside `$( )` is made in a subshell and lost. A named log that
# has already been deleted is worse than no log line at all — it reads as an
# answer. Reached only on the failing path, so a successful run still leaves
# nothing behind.
CHILD_LOG="$(command sed -n 's/^log:[[:space:]][[:space:]]*//p' "$ERRLOG" 2>/dev/null \
             | command sed -n 1p | command awk '{print $1}')"
# The file this script HANDED the child (--log) wins over the one the child
# names on stderr. They are the same file whenever both exist; preferring the
# known path is what lets the failing run mark it for keeping, since the EXIT
# trap would otherwise unlink the very file the last line points at.
[ -z "$RUNLOG" ] || [ ! -s "$RUNLOG" ] || CHILD_LOG="$RUNLOG"
if [ -n "$CHILD_LOG" ] && [ -s "$CHILD_LOG" ]; then
  NAMED_LOG="$CHILD_LOG"
  [ "$CHILD_LOG" != "$RUNLOG" ] || KEEP_RUNLOG=1
else
  NAMED_LOG="$ERRLOG"
  KEEP_ERRLOG=1
fi

REASON="$(key FAILED)"
FIX="$(key FIX)"
[ -n "$REASON" ] || REASON="$(command grep -v '^[[:space:]]*$' "$ERRLOG" | command tail -n 1)"
[ -n "$REASON" ] || REASON="exit $RC"
[ -n "$FIX" ] || FIX="$SCRIPT_DIR/wt $VERB ${ARGS[*]} --verbose"
case "$VERB" in
  new)   printf 'error creating worktree: %s\n' "$REASON" ;;
  close) printf 'error closing worktree: %s\n' "$REASON" ;;
  *)     printf 'error: %s\n' "$REASON" ;;
esac
printf 'fix: %s\n' "$FIX"
printf 'log: %s\n' "$NAMED_LOG"

exit "$RC"
